# Strip labels: labeller

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])

alias Giggly.Plot

mtcars = Giggly.Datasets.mtcars()
msleep = Giggly.Datasets.msleep()

render = fn plot ->
  plot
  |> Giggly.to_svg()
  |> IO.iodata_to_binary()
  |> Kino.Image.new(:svg)
end
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`labeller()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/labeller.html), in the order the page gives
them. Each example shows ggplot2's code first and then Giggly's. An example Giggly cannot draw yet
keeps its ggplot2 code and names what it is waiting for, so the page is also a list of what
remains.

A labeller writes the labels in a facet's strips from the values of its columns. `labeller()`
builds one from a labeller for each column, or one for all the rows' strips and one for all the
columns', and a default for the rest; each may be a labeller function, a function on strings, or a
lookup table of the labels to show in place of the values. The result goes to the `labeller`
argument of `facet_grid()` or `facet_wrap()`. Giggly's `Giggly.Plot.facet_grid/2` and
`Giggly.Plot.facet_wrap/3` take a `labeller:` option, and what `labeller()` builds is a keyword
list there: `columns:` a labeller for each column it names, `rows:` and `cols:` one for each side,
and `default:` one for the rest. A labeller is `:value`, `:both`, `:context` or `:wrap`, R's
`label_value()`, `label_both()`, `label_context()` and `label_wrap_gen()`, a function handed each
label, or a map of labels. A plot is described with `Giggly.Plot` and drawn with
`Giggly.to_svg/2`, which returns an SVG document. The setup cell aliases `Giggly.Plot`, so the
cells write `Plot.new/2`, and defines `render`, which draws a plot and shows it below the cell with
`Kino.Image`. The data is `Giggly.Datasets.mtcars/0`, R's `mtcars`: 32 cars from the 1974 *Motor
Trend* US magazine; and `Giggly.Datasets.msleep/0`, ggplot2's `msleep`: the sleep of 83 mammals,
which the setup cell binds to `mtcars` and `msleep`, R's names for them.

## A labeller for each column

```r
p1 <- ggplot(mtcars, aes(x = mpg, y = wt)) + geom_point()

# You can assign different labellers to variables:
p1 + facet_grid(
  vs + am ~ gear,
  labeller = labeller(vs = label_both, am = label_value)
)
```

Giggly gives the facet's rows as `rows:` and its columns as `cols:`, and the labellers of `vs` and
`am` under `columns:`.

```elixir
p1 =
  mtcars
  |> Plot.new(%{x: :mpg, y: :wt})
  |> Plot.geom_point()

p1
|> Plot.facet_grid(
  rows: [:vs, :am],
  cols: :gear,
  labeller: [columns: [vs: :both, am: :value]]
)
|> render.()
```

## A labeller for the rows and one for the columns

```r
# Or whole margins:
p1 + facet_grid(
  vs + am ~ gear,
  labeller = labeller(.rows = label_both, .cols = label_value)
)
```

```elixir
p1
|> Plot.facet_grid(
  rows: [:vs, :am],
  cols: :gear,
  labeller: [rows: :both, cols: :value]
)
|> render.()
```

## A function on strings

`capitalize()` upper-cases the first letter of each label. The strips of the animals whose diet
`msleep` lacks show `NA`, and a missing value is never handed to the function.

```r
# You can supply functions operating on strings:
capitalize <- function(string) {
  substr(string, 1, 1) <- toupper(substr(string, 1, 1))
  string
}
p2 <- ggplot(msleep, aes(x = sleep_total, y = awake)) + geom_point()
p2 + facet_grid(vore ~ conservation, labeller = labeller(vore = capitalize))
```

Giggly's `capitalize` upper-cases the first letter and keeps the rest, as R's does;
`String.capitalize/1` would also lower-case the rest.

```elixir
capitalize = fn string ->
  {first, rest} = String.split_at(string, 1)
  String.upcase(first) <> rest
end

p2 =
  msleep
  |> Plot.new(%{x: :sleep_total, y: :awake})
  |> Plot.geom_point()

p2
|> Plot.facet_grid(rows: :vore, cols: :conservation, labeller: [columns: [vore: capitalize]])
|> render.()
```

## A lookup table

The named vector gives each `conservation` code its long form, and `capitalize()` labels the
other strips. Giggly's lookup table is a map.

```r
# Or use character vectors as lookup tables:
conservation_status <- c(
  cd = "Conservation Dependent",
  en = "Endangered",
  lc = "Least concern",
  nt = "Near Threatened",
  vu = "Vulnerable",
  domesticated = "Domesticated"
)
## Source: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Conservation_status

p2 + facet_grid(vore ~ conservation, labeller = labeller(
  .default = capitalize,
  conservation = conservation_status
))
```

```elixir
conservation_status = %{
  "cd" => "Conservation Dependent",
  "en" => "Endangered",
  "lc" => "Least concern",
  "nt" => "Near Threatened",
  "vu" => "Vulnerable",
  "domesticated" => "Domesticated"
}

p2
|> Plot.facet_grid(
  rows: :vore,
  cols: :conservation,
  labeller: [default: capitalize, columns: [conservation: conservation_status]]
)
|> render.()
```

## Wrapping long labels

```r
# In the following example, we rename the levels to the long form,
# then apply a wrap labeller to the columns to prevent cropped text
idx <- match(msleep$conservation, names(conservation_status))
msleep$conservation2 <- conservation_status[idx]

p3 <- ggplot(msleep, aes(x = sleep_total, y = awake)) + geom_point()
p3 +
  facet_grid(vore ~ conservation2,
    labeller = labeller(conservation2 = label_wrap_gen(10))
  )
```

The first two lines give each animal its conservation status's long form, missing where its code
is, as a new column `conservation2`; the cell does the same in Elixir, looking each code up in
`conservation_status`. `label_wrap_gen(10)` is `{:wrap, width: 10}`, which breaks each label into
lines shorter than 10 characters, between its words.

```elixir
conservation2 =
  Enum.map(msleep[:conservation], fn code -> conservation_status[code] end)

msleep = Keyword.put(msleep, :conservation2, conservation2)

p3 =
  msleep
  |> Plot.new(%{x: :sleep_total, y: :awake})
  |> Plot.geom_point()

p3
|> Plot.facet_grid(
  rows: :vore,
  cols: :conservation2,
  labeller: [columns: [conservation2: {:wrap, width: 10}]]
)
|> render.()
```

## One labeller for several plots

```r
# labeller() is especially useful to act as a global labeller. You
# can set it up once and use it on a range of different plots with
# different facet specifications.

global_labeller <- labeller(
  vore = capitalize,
  conservation = conservation_status,
  conservation2 = label_wrap_gen(10),
  .default = label_both
)

p2 + facet_grid(vore ~ conservation, labeller = global_labeller)
p3 + facet_wrap(~conservation2, labeller = global_labeller)
```

A column the facet does not split on is passed over, so the one labeller serves both plots. Under
`default: :both` the labels `capitalize` and the lookup give are written with their column's name,
`vore: Carni`, while `{:wrap, width: 10}` writes its own, so `conservation2`'s strips show the
wrapped long forms alone.

```elixir
global_labeller = [
  columns: [
    vore: capitalize,
    conservation: conservation_status,
    conservation2: {:wrap, width: 10}
  ],
  default: :both
]

p2
|> Plot.facet_grid(rows: :vore, cols: :conservation, labeller: global_labeller)
|> render.()
```

```elixir
p3
|> Plot.facet_wrap(:conservation2, labeller: global_labeller)
|> render.()
```
